The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `emit_udiv()` is called with `rn` set to `ARM_R0` (r_scratch) and `rm` set to another register. When `rm` is loaded into `ARM_R0` before `rn`, it results in both registers containing the same value, which leads to incorrect behavior during the division operation.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```
Reason:
The fix involves loading `rn` into `ARM_R1` before loading `rm` into `ARM_R0`. This ensures that `ARM_R0` retains its original value (the dividend) while `rm` (the divisor) is loaded into a different register, preventing the accidental overwriting of `ARM_R0` and ensuring correct behavior during the division operation.

